> I instead ran the data through Term::VT102 (a handy Perl module), which
> then allows you to match each row. It works great, except *incredibly*
> slowly. On an ordinary ttyrec, ttygrep2 was at least 800x slower than
> ttygrep. Seven minutes versus half a second. Yeah, that bad.

Sounds about as bad as ipbt's initial reading phase to me. Good to
know ipbt's isn't the only ttyrec-reading technique that suffers
from this :-)

(Actually PuTTY's terminal emulator is one of the fastest I know of
_in normal use_; it's just that ipbt is far from its normal usage
profile. Nearly all of ipbt's reading phase is spent in the terminal
redraw function, which converts the internal data structures into
the final screen appearance; by the nature of ipbt this has to be
called for every frame, whereas PuTTY proper is very careful to call
it at most 50 times a second because the human eye can't perceive
anything much faster than that. And the redraw function turns out to
be surprisingly slow, which never mattered to PuTTY proper but is
critical to ipbt. Additionally, PuTTY's cunning data structures
which speed up the handling of tens of thousands of lines of
scrollback are probably counterproductive when scrollback isn't an
issue and you're working in a 24-line screen only. I occasionally
wonder how much faster ipbt would go if I'd based it on termrec's
terminal code instead of PuTTY's: termrec's seems to be specifically
designed for this type of job.)
